@page "/"
@using TotalGymLogBook.Domain
@inject RailProfileTable Profiles

@*
    The capable tier (docs/adr/0003). Everything here is DERIVED from the same data the shell
    already rendered, and it arrives whenever the runtime finishes booting. The shell above is
    fully usable before this exists -- that is the entire architectural bet.
*@

<h2>Load ladder <span class="tag">derived tier &middot; .NET</span></h2>
<p class="note">
    Computed in <code>TotalGymLogBook.Domain</code> for a @Bodyweight lb trainee on the
    @_profile.LevelCount-notch rail. This is the kind of work that belongs in Blazor: it reads
    the whole ladder, diffs it, and reasons about progression.
</p>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Level</th><th>Angle</th><th>Load</th><th>Step</th><th>Step %</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        @for (var level = 1; level <= _profile.LevelCount; level++)
        {
            var lb = Load(level);
            var step = level == 1 ? (double?)null : lb - Load(level - 1);
            var pct = step is null ? (double?)null : step / Load(level - 1) * 100;

            <tr>
                <td>@level</td>
                <td>@_profile.AngleForLevel(level).ToString("0.0")&deg;</td>
                <td><strong>@lb.ToString("0.0")</strong></td>
                <td>@(step is null ? "—" : "+" + step.Value.ToString("0.0"))</td>
                <td class="@(pct > 15 ? "coarse" : "")">
                    @(pct is null ? "—" : pct.Value.ToString("0.0") + "%")
                </td>
            </tr>
        }
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="note">
    Steps are near-uniform in pounds but not in percentage — highlighted rows are jumps over
    15%, where the coach should micro-step with added weight instead of a level change
    (docs/adr/0004).
</p>

<style>
    .tg-derived { max-width: 34rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1rem 3rem; }
    .tg-derived h2 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 2rem 0 .25rem; }
    .tag {
        display: inline-block; font-size: .6875rem; letter-spacing: .04em; text-transform: uppercase;
        background: #6b7280; color: #fff; border-radius: 999px; padding: .15rem .5rem;
        vertical-align: middle;
    }
    .tg-derived .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: .8125rem; }
    .tg-derived table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
    .tg-derived th, .tg-derived td {
        text-align: right; padding: .3rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
        font-size: .875rem;
    }
    .tg-derived th:first-child, .tg-derived td:first-child { text-align: left; }
    .tg-derived th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 500; font-size: .75rem; }
    .tg-derived td.coarse { color: #d97706; font-weight: 600; }
</style>

@code {
    private const double Bodyweight = 180;

    private RailProfile _profile = default!;

    protected override void OnInitialized() => _profile = Profiles.ForLevelCount(14);

    private double Load(int level) => ResistanceCalculator.Compute(
        _profile, new ResistanceInputs { BodyweightLb = Bodyweight, Level = level });
}
